---
import Base from '../layouts/Base.astro';
import Prose from '../components/Prose.astro';

const title = 'Privacy — Reza Bina';
const description =
  'How this site handles analytics: cookieless, aggregate, no personal data. What is counted, what is not, and who processes it.';
---

<Base title={title} description={description}>
  <article class="wrap privacy">
    <h1 class="privacy__title">privacy</h1>

    <Prose>
      <p>
        I like to know which posts people actually read, and roughly where they came from, so I can
        write more of what turns out to be useful. That is the entire reason this site has analytics.
        This page says exactly what that means, because a site that collects anything at all owes you
        the specifics rather than a reassuring word.
      </p>

      <p>
        What gets counted is aggregate and shallow: a page view, the site that referred you (a search
        engine, a link, a post somewhere), and a rough country and browser. That is it. Numbers in a
        dashboard, not a profile of you.
      </p>

      <p>
        What does not happen: no cookies, so there is no banner to click through. No personal data.
        No cross-site tracking, no advertising, no fingerprinting, no attempt to work out who you are
        or to follow you anywhere else. Nothing is stored that could single you out.
      </p>

      <p>
        The counting is done by <a href="https://www.goatcounter.com" rel="noopener">GoatCounter</a>,
        an open-source, privacy-first analytics service that is built to collect the aggregate numbers
        above and nothing more. I chose it precisely because it is the only kind of analytics that is
        consistent with how I build everything else here, and with the apps: no accounts, no tracking,
        nothing that needs to leave your device to be trusted.
      </p>

      <p>
        If that ever stops being true, this page changes in the same breath. Questions are welcome at
        <a href="mailto:rezabina.dev@gmail.com">rezabina.dev@gmail.com</a>.
      </p>
    </Prose>
  </article>
</Base>

<style>
  .privacy {
    /* A page title is a bigger event than a section: 56, not 40. */
    margin-top: 56px;
  }

  /* A page title: weight and colour, not a section label — and no # marker,
     which means "section" and nothing else. */
  .privacy__title {
    font-size: 14px;
    line-height: 1.7;
    font-weight: 600;
    color: var(--ink);
    margin-bottom: 12px;
  }
</style>
